1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:14,001
It was the world's first specialized gun, created for the upper class and designed to shoot birds for sport.

2
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
But its reputation for reliability and durability led to much greater roles.

3
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Hunter, Protector and Warrior.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,200
For more than four centuries, if a man could afford to own just one weapon, he chose the shotgun.

5
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:11,120
On the frontier, or the battlefield,

6
00:01:11,660 --> 00:01:14,760
it was one of the most frightening sounds ever heard.

7
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:20,400
It's machinery in motion, and it's the machinery of death in motion,

8
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:22,420
and it sounds like nothing else.

9
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:27,540
Powerful enough to strike fear into the bravest soldier

10
00:01:27,541 --> 00:01:29,540
or the minnest outlaw.

11
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:35,320
No one's going to walk into a darkened room knowing that that Clatch Clatch is in there.

12
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:41,480
That's because those facing the barrel end of a shotgun knew what was coming next.

13
00:01:49,700 --> 00:01:54,340
Ease of use and versatility are the qualities that made the shotgun popular,

14
00:01:54,341 --> 00:01:59,660
but the ability to fire a lethal spray of ammunition with a single pull of the trigger

15
00:01:59,661 --> 00:02:01,660
made it legendary.

16
00:02:07,660 --> 00:02:09,660
America's earliest settlers used shotguns

17
00:02:09,661 --> 00:02:11,660
to carve out a life in the New World.

18
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:16,660
Outlaws used them to rob their way across the Western frontier.

19
00:02:17,660 --> 00:02:20,660
Soldiers carried them in the trenches on the Western Front.

20
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:24,660
And today, almost every American police car

21
00:02:24,661 --> 00:02:26,660
is equipped with a shotgun.

22
00:02:26,661 --> 00:02:33,660
No other type of firearm can boast such wide-ranging and long-standing acceptance.

23
00:02:41,660 --> 00:02:47,660
Handheld, smoothbore guns first appeared in Europe during the early 14th century.

24
00:02:47,661 --> 00:02:53,660
But these tended to be either large, unwieldy muskets or small cannon-like weapons.

25
00:02:53,661 --> 00:02:55,620
cannon-like weapons.

26
00:02:55,621 --> 00:02:58,140
Most were ineffective against anything other

27
00:02:58,141 --> 00:03:00,340
than a stationary target.

28
00:03:00,341 --> 00:03:01,500
Get, fire!

29
00:03:05,140 --> 00:03:07,980
Sometime in the early 15th century,

30
00:03:07,981 --> 00:03:12,220
Europe's aristocracy discovered sports shooting.

31
00:03:12,221 --> 00:03:15,220
At the time, hunting small game with trained falcons

32
00:03:15,221 --> 00:03:18,820
was highly popular in royal circles.

33
00:03:18,821 --> 00:03:22,380
Some say that a French nobleman found a novel use

34
00:03:22,381 --> 00:03:25,340
his gun, quite by accident.

35
00:03:26,460 --> 00:03:29,680
Probably somebody was out with their falcons

36
00:03:29,681 --> 00:03:33,220
and they said, well, I might as well take along my gun too,

37
00:03:33,221 --> 00:03:36,280
you know, and the falcon didn't fly that day,

38
00:03:36,281 --> 00:03:39,000
it was sick or something, and the duck flew

39
00:03:39,001 --> 00:03:42,140
or the pheasant or whatever, the grouse or whatever,

40
00:03:42,141 --> 00:03:46,820
and the guy shot and the game fell down and he said,

41
00:03:46,821 --> 00:03:49,500
wait a second, I don't even have to chase the falcon

42
00:03:49,501 --> 00:03:51,580
to get it back, you know, this is really good.

43
00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:59,380
But bringing down a bird in flight with a ball fired by an early musket was largely a matter of luck.

44
00:03:59,381 --> 00:04:04,380
Hunters found a way to increase their chances in the cannon of the day.

45
00:04:04,381 --> 00:04:12,380
The big guns were often loaded with small lead balls, which, after being fired, spread out in a wide pattern.

46
00:04:12,381 --> 00:04:15,380
The same principle worked with muskets.

47
00:04:15,381 --> 00:04:21,380
Noblemen began experimenting with different quantities of small pellets, known as shot.

48
00:04:22,380 --> 00:04:32,820
When the musket proved awkward for quick aiming, gun makers produced longer, lighter guns,

49
00:04:32,821 --> 00:04:36,000
which allowed for more maneuverability.

50
00:04:36,001 --> 00:04:41,480
These weapons, designed to fire shot only, were called Foulers.

51
00:04:41,481 --> 00:04:48,300
These early shotguns were the first hand-held firearms made for a specific purpose.

52
00:04:48,301 --> 00:04:51,340
This was used for one purpose and one purpose alone,

53
00:04:51,341 --> 00:04:53,880
which was to shoot birds.

54
00:04:53,881 --> 00:04:55,540
This is a bird hunting gun.

55
00:04:55,541 --> 00:04:57,040
Yes, you could use it for rabbits,

56
00:04:57,041 --> 00:04:58,680
and you could use it for other small game,

57
00:04:58,681 --> 00:05:02,240
but it was specifically made as a fouling piece.

58
00:05:03,820 --> 00:05:06,160
Fouling guns and the sport of shooting

59
00:05:06,161 --> 00:05:11,100
were introduced to England in 1660 by King Charles II.

60
00:05:11,101 --> 00:05:13,220
When he returned from exile in France

61
00:05:13,221 --> 00:05:15,100
after England's Civil War,

62
00:05:15,101 --> 00:05:17,660
Charles brought along his new passion.

63
00:05:18,300 --> 00:05:23,940
Noblemen in both Great Britain and the continent used to vie with each other.

64
00:05:23,941 --> 00:05:30,800
This was the sport of noblemen and the sport of kings was to shoot a flying animal out of the air

65
00:05:30,801 --> 00:05:35,080
with a lot of pellets that came from the smoothbore of a shotgun.

66
00:05:35,560 --> 00:05:37,080
There were books written on the subject.

67
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:39,980
I believe the earliest one is dated 1728.

68
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:43,740
It's called Pteroplegia or The Art of Shooting Flying.

69
00:05:43,741 --> 00:05:52,260
One distinctive feature of the early fowler was its long, slender barrel, which could measure up to six feet in length.

70
00:05:53,860 --> 00:06:01,220
Gunmakers of the time believed this increased a gun's firing distance, but fowlers were meant to impress as well as shoot.

71
00:06:04,280 --> 00:06:08,420
This is a typical example of a late 17th century fowling piece.

72
00:06:08,421 --> 00:06:11,060
This one happens to be English by Thomas Warnall,

73
00:06:11,280 --> 00:06:13,740
and it's got the typical decoration and form

74
00:06:13,741 --> 00:06:17,480
and the extremely long barrel of a fouling piece of that era.

75
00:06:17,940 --> 00:06:20,680
It has this lovely serpentine side plate,

76
00:06:20,780 --> 00:06:24,160
which is typical of guns from England and the continent,

77
00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:27,060
and it has the lovely chiseling on the barrel.

78
00:06:27,500 --> 00:06:31,920
It's also chiseled on the front of the frisson

79
00:06:31,921 --> 00:06:33,960
as well as the hammer.

80
00:06:33,961 --> 00:06:42,720
The beauty and elegance of handcrafted fowlers was mirrored in the spectacular shooting parties,

81
00:06:42,940 --> 00:06:45,760
which became an important part of the social calendar.

82
00:06:47,160 --> 00:06:52,760
They had huge parties involving some tens or twenties of people and hundreds of beaters,

83
00:06:52,920 --> 00:06:58,720
as they called them, and going through the bush and trying to flush game out

84
00:06:58,721 --> 00:07:01,680
so these noblemen could shoot them while they were flying.

85
00:07:01,681 --> 00:07:06,680
Though created for the upper class, Fowlers were soon available to the masses.

86
00:07:06,681 --> 00:07:14,680
They adapted less expensive versions as well as other smoothbore guns to fit their own more practical needs.

87
00:07:16,680 --> 00:07:27,680
The common people would use them to hunt for food for the family or to help keep their livestock safe from marauding animals or defence in their home.

88
00:07:27,681 --> 00:07:33,011
home. Before long, variations of the furlough began to appear.

89
00:07:33,012 --> 00:07:35,160
For instance, the blunder

90
00:07:35,161 --> 00:07:40,060
bus was designed not for sport, but for self-protection and for war.

91
00:07:40,061 --> 00:07:43,163
They were loaded with pellets.

92
00:07:43,164 --> 00:07:49,060
They were essentially the first sawn off or riot shotgun,

93
00:07:49,061 --> 00:07:52,107
and they were commonly used on ships.

94
00:07:52,108 --> 00:07:56,060
When they were in battle, they were required use

95
00:07:56,061 --> 00:08:00,140
on Her Majesty's naval vessels during the Napoleonic Wars

96
00:08:00,141 --> 00:08:02,780
and earlier because they were so effective

97
00:08:02,781 --> 00:08:05,240
against opponents that were in the top sails.

98
00:08:06,840 --> 00:08:11,120
In less than 50 years, the Fowler had outgrown its origins.

99
00:08:11,860 --> 00:08:15,080
Its small pellet ammunition had also inspired a new name,

100
00:08:15,880 --> 00:08:16,580
the shotgun.

101
00:08:17,980 --> 00:08:20,920
And although Europeans had perfected the weapon,

102
00:08:21,380 --> 00:08:24,520
it was in America that people came to trust their lives

103
00:08:24,521 --> 00:08:25,300
to the shotgun.

104
00:08:26,060 --> 00:08:34,920
The first British settlers in the New World found it filled with adventures, opportunities, and unpredictable dangers.

105
00:08:35,820 --> 00:08:41,380
When the Pilgrims arrived in 1620, the shotgun was an essential tool for survival.

106
00:08:45,460 --> 00:08:52,300
They were advised to bring shotguns because of their total practicality and the fact that America at the time was teeming with game.

107
00:08:52,301 --> 00:08:55,980
It was like a giant supermarket in terms of game,

108
00:08:56,120 --> 00:08:59,700
and a shotgun was the most effective weapon for harvesting that game.

109
00:09:01,920 --> 00:09:04,220
Shotguns fascinated the Native Americans,

110
00:09:04,520 --> 00:09:08,960
who were amazed by the pilgrims' ability to easily shoot birds out of the sky.

111
00:09:12,500 --> 00:09:15,040
They desired one of our men to shoot a crow,

112
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:18,520
complaining what damage they sustained in their corn by them.

113
00:09:18,700 --> 00:09:22,140
They much admired it, as other shots on other occasions.

114
00:09:22,300 --> 00:09:27,300
Edward Winslow, Plymouth, Massachusetts.

115
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:31,400
Many of the early colonists were poor.

116
00:09:31,401 --> 00:09:34,600
They were escaping economic hardships in the old world

117
00:09:34,601 --> 00:09:37,520
and could rarely afford more than one weapon.

118
00:09:37,521 --> 00:09:41,560
The gun they owned had to be able to put food on the table.

119
00:09:43,320 --> 00:09:45,420
You do not necessarily want to go out

120
00:09:45,421 --> 00:09:47,740
and kill a deer every day.

121
00:09:47,741 --> 00:09:49,380
Without refrigeration, you don't have a way

122
00:09:49,381 --> 00:09:51,180
to keep the meat, but you do need

123
00:09:51,181 --> 00:09:52,520
to feed your family every day.

124
00:09:52,521 --> 00:09:55,860
So the shotgun was the number one weapon

125
00:09:55,861 --> 00:09:58,420
that was used to take care of feeding their families.

126
00:10:00,980 --> 00:10:03,140
The shotguns favored by the Americans

127
00:10:03,141 --> 00:10:05,400
in the 17th and 18th centuries

128
00:10:05,401 --> 00:10:08,100
were mostly large caliber, single barrel,

129
00:10:08,101 --> 00:10:10,320
muzzle-loading flintlocks.

130
00:10:10,321 --> 00:10:12,340
These multi-purpose guns could handle

131
00:10:12,341 --> 00:10:14,060
a variety of ammunition.

132
00:10:17,560 --> 00:10:20,000
One large caliber ball was used for deer

133
00:10:20,001 --> 00:10:26,220
other big game. Several medium caliber shot for wild pigs and the like, or a

134
00:10:26,221 --> 00:10:33,440
larger number of shot for birds and smaller game. Shotguns also provided

135
00:10:33,441 --> 00:10:38,600
protection. The deadly combination of a large swarm of shot and a heavy powder

136
00:10:38,601 --> 00:10:45,040
charge resulted in far greater damage and ball firing muskets.

137
00:10:45,400 --> 00:10:49,680
If you are using a shotgun rather than a single projectile you have a much better

138
00:10:49,681 --> 00:10:56,680
chance of hitting your target because you put out, even at 50 yards, a pretty good pattern

139
00:10:57,920 --> 00:11:01,860
and you don't have to be dead accurate.

140
00:11:01,861 --> 00:11:06,880
For those who relied upon shotguns for survival, several advancements helped increase their

141
00:11:06,881 --> 00:11:10,620
chances.

142
00:11:10,621 --> 00:11:17,820
In the late 1700s, improvements in both metallurgy and gunpowder gave birth to shorter and lighter

143
00:11:17,821 --> 00:11:19,040
guns.

144
00:11:19,041 --> 00:11:23,540
The gun makers began to add a second barrel and trigger to the fouler.

145
00:11:23,541 --> 00:11:30,160
The barrels could be fired simultaneously or one at a time.

146
00:11:30,161 --> 00:11:34,880
This is the design that will dominate shotguns through most of its history and this is the

147
00:11:34,881 --> 00:11:38,360
most useful design in shotgun invention.

148
00:11:38,361 --> 00:11:44,760
Whether it be side by side or over and under, two barrels and two tube shotguns are going

149
00:11:44,761 --> 00:11:48,360
to pretty much dominate shotgun history.

150
00:11:49,040 --> 00:11:55,360
In 1806, Frenchman Joseph Manton patented the elevated sighting rib.

151
00:11:56,040 --> 00:12:02,140
This metal strip both strengthened the double-barrelled guns and aided in their long-range accuracy.

152
00:12:03,800 --> 00:12:13,260
The elevated rib served as a perfect plane for which to sight and fire at a flying or moving bird.

153
00:12:13,261 --> 00:12:20,900
and this elevated rib combined with this little German silver front sight

154
00:12:20,901 --> 00:12:25,540
made this a very fine fouling piece indeed,

155
00:12:25,840 --> 00:12:34,340
although, as I say, well within the reach of anybody who had any sort of decent employment.

156
00:12:36,020 --> 00:12:39,500
Though single-barrel shotguns were widely used,

157
00:12:39,501 --> 00:12:42,920
the double-barrel was preferred by America's frontiersmen,

158
00:12:43,260 --> 00:12:48,040
In the wilderness, two barrels almost always prove better than one.

159
00:12:49,240 --> 00:12:54,340
One of the advantages of a double-barrel shotgun, especially in hostile country,

160
00:12:54,900 --> 00:12:59,300
is that you could load two barrels with different loads.

161
00:12:59,700 --> 00:13:02,780
One you might load for hunting small game,

162
00:13:02,781 --> 00:13:07,380
and the other you could keep ready with buck and ball or more powerful charge

163
00:13:07,381 --> 00:13:10,760
in case you were attacked, thus protecting yourself.

164
00:13:10,761 --> 00:13:19,800
Legendary Americans like Jim Bridger, Jedediah Smith, and James Beckworth are reported to

165
00:13:19,801 --> 00:13:21,340
have used shotguns.

166
00:13:22,700 --> 00:13:29,740
Well, the mountain man was a wonderful character because he was totally free, and he bet on

167
00:13:29,741 --> 00:13:30,480
his own wits.

168
00:13:30,980 --> 00:13:36,460
You always hear of the rifles, and that was very important to shoot buffalo or grizzly

169
00:13:36,461 --> 00:13:37,640
bears or things like that.

170
00:13:37,641 --> 00:13:40,880
but probably the gun that was always around the saddle horn,

171
00:13:41,520 --> 00:13:44,720
hanging off the saddle horn of a mountain man's horse, was his shotgun.

172
00:13:45,720 --> 00:13:48,480
Because that could be immediately used,

173
00:13:49,180 --> 00:13:52,920
and it could get him all kinds of game.

174
00:13:53,680 --> 00:13:57,280
And his first requirement was to eat.

175
00:13:58,360 --> 00:14:03,360
The double-barreled shotgun was also well-suited for short-range defense.

176
00:14:03,361 --> 00:14:07,360
The ability to fire two tremendous blasts of ammunition

177
00:14:07,361 --> 00:14:10,360
made it far preferable to a single bullet

178
00:14:10,361 --> 00:14:12,360
or ball-firing weapon.

179
00:14:17,360 --> 00:14:20,360
This police video demonstrates the shotgun's power.

180
00:14:20,361 --> 00:14:25,360
At 15 feet, the ammunition spray forms a tight pattern.

181
00:14:25,361 --> 00:14:27,360
As the distance is increased,

182
00:14:27,361 --> 00:14:29,360
the pellets spread out roughly one inch

183
00:14:29,361 --> 00:14:31,360
for every one yard travelled.

184
00:14:31,361 --> 00:14:38,360
travelled. The shotgun is most effective within 50 yards of a target.

185
00:14:38,361 --> 00:14:46,360
Many people have the mistaken belief that the moment it's fired the shot is spread to

186
00:14:46,361 --> 00:14:47,232
a wide pattern.

187
00:14:47,233 --> 00:14:51,360
They see this in movies and they see walls being blown apart and so on.

188
00:14:51,361 --> 00:14:56,360
That's not necessarily the case. If you're too close you're not going to get a large

189
00:14:56,361 --> 00:14:57,116
shot pattern.

190
00:14:57,117 --> 00:15:01,360
However, the opponent is going to be hit with a heck of a wallop of shot.

191
00:15:04,360 --> 00:15:08,200
By the late 1830s, Casimir Lefecher, a Frenchman,

192
00:15:08,201 --> 00:15:10,680
had revolutionized the shotgun.

193
00:15:10,681 --> 00:15:13,480
He combined a break-open shotgun design

194
00:15:13,481 --> 00:15:16,400
with ammunition-filled cartridges.

195
00:15:16,401 --> 00:15:18,680
The barrel separated from the stock

196
00:15:18,681 --> 00:15:23,000
by pivoting on an under hinge near the trigger.

197
00:15:23,001 --> 00:15:25,720
Ammunition was then inserted at the breach.

198
00:15:25,721 --> 00:15:33,700
The shotgun's most celebrated period came during the settling of America's Wild West.

199
00:15:34,300 --> 00:15:39,060
From the 1860s through the 1890s, from St. Louis to San Francisco,

200
00:15:39,600 --> 00:15:42,680
it seemed as if everyone carried a double-barrel shotgun.

201
00:15:46,280 --> 00:15:55,080
Immigrants, farmers, ranchers, lawmen, outlaws, military men, Indians, you name it.

202
00:15:55,081 --> 00:15:59,540
Just about everybody of some sort would use a shotgun if they could get one.

203
00:15:59,620 --> 00:16:01,080
It was the most versatile weapon.

204
00:16:01,520 --> 00:16:04,260
If you only had one gun and you had to go west,

205
00:16:04,520 --> 00:16:07,060
you would be best served with a double-barreled shotgun.

206
00:16:08,200 --> 00:16:12,300
Like many of their forefathers, Western settlers were often poor

207
00:16:12,301 --> 00:16:14,260
and could only afford one gun.

208
00:16:14,900 --> 00:16:18,340
The shotgun served as both protector and hunter.

209
00:16:18,341 --> 00:16:26,740
Many immigrants travelled west with a shotgun stashed behind the seat of their covered wagon.

210
00:16:27,380 --> 00:16:29,760
It was much easier to handle than a rifle.

211
00:16:30,620 --> 00:16:35,960
This was essential for families where a weapon was used by men, women and children.

212
00:16:36,740 --> 00:16:41,260
Anybody who fires guns will tell you that it's very, very hard to hit, very easy to miss.

213
00:16:41,820 --> 00:16:47,860
But if you have a shotgun, it's much easier to hit because you have a pattern of lead flying through the air.

214
00:16:47,861 --> 00:16:55,380
And so it becomes an excellent choice for a weapon for someone that doesn't have time to train, doesn't have time to practice.

215
00:16:57,100 --> 00:17:00,300
The shotgun's durability was also crucial.

216
00:17:01,020 --> 00:17:06,560
Its hollow tube was easier to clean and maintain than the groove barrels found in a pistol or rifle.

217
00:17:07,360 --> 00:17:14,420
Also, its relatively simple mechanism meant most breakdowns could be fixed on the spot without the need of a gunsmith.

218
00:17:14,421 --> 00:17:23,680
Simplicity and economics and the ability to hit well without a lot of practice all make the

219
00:17:23,681 --> 00:17:31,040
shotgun an extremely good good firearm and it's the best firearm ever made for hunting game

220
00:17:31,041 --> 00:17:32,951
small game and birds.

221
00:17:32,952 --> 00:17:39,960
But on the lawless frontier the need for protection was often as important as

222
00:17:39,961 --> 00:17:40,674
eating.

223
00:17:40,675 --> 00:17:49,860
In boom towns and cow towns across the west, gold and currency were transported on special

224
00:17:49,861 --> 00:17:51,833
money wagons or stagecoaches.

225
00:17:51,834 --> 00:17:56,460
Shotguns were the primary weapons used to safeguard these shipments.

226
00:17:57,400 --> 00:18:01,663
Riding shotgun meant more than just sitting in the front seat.

227
00:18:01,664 --> 00:18:04,000
This is an example of a historical

228
00:18:04,001 --> 00:18:07,740
old coach gun, double-barreled, exposed hammers.

229
00:18:08,700 --> 00:18:11,100
These were usually 10-gauge or 12-gauge shotguns.

230
00:18:11,500 --> 00:18:14,300
And they were cut down, sawed off, short barrel.

231
00:18:15,940 --> 00:18:20,060
Some sawed-off shotguns had barrels cut as short as 9 inches.

232
00:18:20,800 --> 00:18:23,420
This increased the recoil effect, or kick,

233
00:18:23,820 --> 00:18:26,400
but made the guns much more maneuverable.

234
00:18:28,040 --> 00:18:31,380
A sawed-off shotgun was much more preferable

235
00:18:31,381 --> 00:18:33,660
to a long-barreled shotgun, as you can see here.

236
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:38,140
just the easiness to wield these things around,

237
00:18:38,141 --> 00:18:43,140
it's much easier to wield a sawed-off shotgun around.

238
00:18:43,780 --> 00:18:46,720
Matter of fact, one stagecoach driver said that

239
00:18:46,721 --> 00:18:50,480
when bouncing on a coach and being pursued by road agents,

240
00:18:50,481 --> 00:18:53,320
it was a lot easier to turn and get off

241
00:18:53,321 --> 00:18:56,520
relatively accurate shots with a short-barreled shotgun

242
00:18:56,521 --> 00:19:00,900
as opposed to moving around one of these big long toms.

243
00:19:00,901 --> 00:19:05,900
Stagecoach drivers often faced the other end of the barrel.

244
00:19:05,901 --> 00:19:09,900
Highway bandits and train robbers also favored the shotgun.

245
00:19:13,900 --> 00:19:16,900
The notorious Black Bart, whose exploits were immortalized

246
00:19:16,901 --> 00:19:20,900
in early Hollywood, held up over 20 stagecoaches

247
00:19:20,901 --> 00:19:21,900
with a shotgun.

248
00:19:21,901 --> 00:19:22,888
shotgun.

249
00:19:22,889 --> 00:19:32,520
Black Bart said that he had never had his shotgun loaded and probably wouldn't

250
00:19:32,521 --> 00:19:38,500
even work, but he knew that it had the intimidation factor, that when he leveled that shotgun

251
00:19:38,501 --> 00:19:42,980
at a stagecoach driver that they were going to listen to him and transact the business

252
00:19:42,981 --> 00:19:43,745
at hand.

253
00:19:43,746 --> 00:19:51,400
Lawmen like Wild Bill Hickok, Wyatt Earp, and Bat Masterson used double-barreled

254
00:19:51,401 --> 00:19:52,101
shotguns.

255
00:19:52,102 --> 00:19:57,100
When the cattle drives ended and the cowboys went wild, it was the lawman's

256
00:19:57,101 --> 00:19:59,100
job to keep the peace.

257
00:19:59,101 --> 00:20:05,900
What was great about the shotgun was that if you had to deal with crowds, if you had

258
00:20:05,901 --> 00:20:10,700
to deal with people who were coming in numbers, there was no question in the minds of the

259
00:20:10,701 --> 00:20:15,700
people you were facing that you were not going to miss. And that was critical.

260
00:20:15,701 --> 00:20:23,700
Contrary to Hollywood lore, in real Old West shootouts, the shotgun proved far superior

261
00:20:23,701 --> 00:20:25,700
to the more celebrated rifle.

262
00:20:25,701 --> 00:20:31,900
If you're a police officer and you know there's trouble and you're expecting problems, the

263
00:20:31,901 --> 00:20:36,000
first thing you do is go into your office, get the shotgun and head back out on the streets.

264
00:20:36,001 --> 00:20:40,700
You then could hide behind a building or take cover and you could take in from behind a

265
00:20:40,701 --> 00:20:41,700
building towards people moving.

266
00:20:41,701 --> 00:20:44,900
and when people are moving, the shotgun is much more

267
00:20:44,901 --> 00:20:48,340
effective in a battle than a single projectile

268
00:20:48,341 --> 00:20:49,480
out of a rifle.

269
00:20:52,240 --> 00:20:55,780
Bill Doolin, a member of the infamous Doolin-Dolton gang,

270
00:20:55,781 --> 00:20:59,120
learned of the shotgun's power the hard way.

271
00:20:59,121 --> 00:21:02,220
After escaping from jail, Doolin was chased down

272
00:21:02,221 --> 00:21:05,020
and killed in a shootout with a sheriff's posse.

273
00:21:05,021 --> 00:21:17,320
In the most famous shootout of all, the gunfight at the OK Corral, the shotgun demonstrated

274
00:21:17,321 --> 00:21:20,020
its power over pistols.

275
00:21:20,021 --> 00:21:25,640
The men facing each other carried heavy caliber revolvers, but Doc Holliday also wielded a

276
00:21:25,641 --> 00:21:27,500
shotgun.

277
00:21:27,501 --> 00:21:32,820
During the 30-second shootout, roughly 30 shots were fired, including a double-barrel

278
00:21:32,821 --> 00:21:36,420
blast from the shotgun.

279
00:21:36,421 --> 00:21:42,660
Ironically, while the pistols that were used were heavy caliber pistols and were very effective,

280
00:21:42,661 --> 00:21:49,440
many of the men shot there were still able to get off follow-up shots afterwards despite

281
00:21:49,441 --> 00:21:51,400
being mortally wounded.

282
00:21:51,401 --> 00:21:55,980
But this shotgun blast that Holliday fired was just effective.

283
00:21:55,981 --> 00:22:01,220
You got three men that were killed and three men that were wounded.

284
00:22:01,221 --> 00:22:03,260
and it took 30 shots.

285
00:22:04,180 --> 00:22:06,520
On the other hand, with the shotgun,

286
00:22:06,521 --> 00:22:08,380
they didn't have any misses.

287
00:22:08,381 --> 00:22:11,220
So of all of the shots in the Gunfight at the OK Corral,

288
00:22:11,221 --> 00:22:13,820
the most effective shots came out of a shotgun.

289
00:22:13,821 --> 00:22:15,820
They were the ones that didn't miss.

290
00:22:17,060 --> 00:22:19,360
The shotgun later earned a permanent place

291
00:22:19,361 --> 00:22:23,580
in Western folklore through Buffalo Bill's Wild West shows.

292
00:22:23,581 --> 00:22:27,440
Sharpshooters like Annie Oakley and Johnny Baker

293
00:22:27,441 --> 00:22:30,740
thrilled worldwide audiences with their amazing ability

294
00:22:30,741 --> 00:22:33,660
to shoot glass balls thrown into the air.

295
00:22:37,760 --> 00:22:41,580
While the double-barreled shotgun dominated the 19th century,

296
00:22:41,581 --> 00:22:45,580
gun designers continually sought ways to increase its power.

297
00:22:51,120 --> 00:22:53,740
The revolving shotgun has been in existence

298
00:22:53,741 --> 00:22:55,860
since the late 1700s.

299
00:22:55,861 --> 00:22:58,920
It could fire five rounds before reloading,

300
00:22:58,921 --> 00:23:01,660
but was much more expensive than the double barrels

301
00:23:01,661 --> 00:23:02,920
and never caught on.

302
00:23:04,860 --> 00:23:09,320
In 1882, Christopher Spencer, along with Sylvester Roper,

303
00:23:09,321 --> 00:23:13,200
created the first pump or slide action shotgun.

304
00:23:13,201 --> 00:23:15,460
Their revolutionary design failed,

305
00:23:15,461 --> 00:23:17,640
but fathered many imitators.

306
00:23:19,900 --> 00:23:23,660
A decade later, legendary gun designer John Browning

307
00:23:23,661 --> 00:23:27,160
helped usher in a new era for the shotgun.

308
00:23:27,161 --> 00:23:30,500
Browning's six-shot Winchester Model 1887

309
00:23:30,501 --> 00:23:34,120
became the first commercially successful repeating shotgun.

310
00:23:37,640 --> 00:23:40,700
This was a shotgun that had a lever action,

311
00:23:40,701 --> 00:23:43,200
not unlike the Winchester rifles.

312
00:23:43,201 --> 00:23:45,060
And when you loaded the shotgun,

313
00:23:46,280 --> 00:23:50,840
you made the lever engage and you loaded very quickly.

314
00:23:50,841 --> 00:23:54,360
So this allowed you to carry a shotgun

315
00:23:54,361 --> 00:23:56,080
that was very much like a rifle,

316
00:23:56,081 --> 00:23:58,420
in fact that you could shoot multiple times

317
00:23:58,421 --> 00:24:01,400
and still have the effectiveness of the shotgun

318
00:24:01,401 --> 00:24:04,300
when it leaves the muzzle, when it shoots.

319
00:24:04,301 --> 00:24:06,720
It was combining the best properties of a rifle

320
00:24:06,721 --> 00:24:08,680
and the best properties of a shotgun.

321
00:24:10,920 --> 00:24:12,680
Browning repeated his success

322
00:24:12,681 --> 00:24:15,200
with the six-shot Winchester pump action,

323
00:24:15,201 --> 00:24:20,200
first the model 1893 and later the 1897.

324
00:24:26,080 --> 00:24:31,080
Lawmen across America fell in love with its power.

325
00:24:40,200 --> 00:24:43,160
So did a legion of outlaws from Butch Cassidy

326
00:24:43,161 --> 00:24:45,500
and the Sundance Kid in the 1880s

327
00:24:45,501 --> 00:24:48,340
to Bonnie and Clyde in the 1930s.

328
00:24:50,080 --> 00:24:53,360
For one thing, this shotgun has a distinctive sound.

329
00:24:53,361 --> 00:24:57,360
This shotgun is a weapon that every outlaw

330
00:24:57,361 --> 00:25:00,260
and every burglar knows, because as soon as that shotgun

331
00:25:00,261 --> 00:25:05,260
is made ready to fire, it makes a sound that is distinctive.

332
00:25:05,720 --> 00:25:09,400
And it's very, very efficient, very, very quick,

333
00:25:09,401 --> 00:25:13,400
and you can fire five, six shots very, very rapidly.

334
00:25:14,820 --> 00:25:16,260
It's a sound like none other.

335
00:25:16,261 --> 00:25:28,960
It's been popularized on television and movies for decades now, and it sounds like nothing

336
00:25:28,961 --> 00:25:30,560
else.

337
00:25:30,561 --> 00:25:40,860
There was a story of a man that got into an altercation with another man, and the first

338
00:25:40,861 --> 00:25:44,820
fellow said, don't mess with me, I'm a master at karate.

339
00:25:44,821 --> 00:25:47,220
and the other fellow said, I'm a master at kachuk.

340
00:25:47,221 --> 00:25:48,160
And he said, what's that?

341
00:25:48,161 --> 00:25:50,460
And he pulled out a shotgun and went, kachuk.

342
00:25:50,461 --> 00:25:52,000
And that ended the fight right there.

343
00:25:55,840 --> 00:25:59,160
The pump action was the shape of the future.

344
00:25:59,161 --> 00:26:03,500
In the 20th century, the shotgun would continue to flourish.

345
00:26:03,501 --> 00:26:17,501
From its origins as a plaything for European royalty, the shotgun emerged as an essential

346
00:26:19,540 --> 00:26:23,560
tool for the common man on the American frontier.

347
00:26:23,561 --> 00:26:28,500
Through the centuries, it also gained a fearsome reputation as a military weapon.

348
00:26:28,501 --> 00:26:41,300
During the American Revolution, General George Washington encouraged his troops to load their

349
00:26:41,301 --> 00:26:46,620
muskets with one lead ball along with several small shot pellets.

350
00:26:46,621 --> 00:26:52,860
This combination known as buck and ball increased firepower as well as the chances of hitting

351
00:26:52,861 --> 00:26:56,620
the enemy.

352
00:26:56,621 --> 00:27:03,560
Washington was a proponent of the use of buck and ball in the muskets because in close fighting

353
00:27:03,561 --> 00:27:07,198
this would give his troops a real advantage over the enemy.

354
00:27:07,199 --> 00:27:08,740
The devastation caused by

355
00:27:08,741 --> 00:27:17,400
round ball and small buckshot pellets could be very, very effective fighting against a

356
00:27:17,401 --> 00:27:23,560
force of British soldiers coming at them.

357
00:27:23,561 --> 00:27:28,520
Rock and ball became standard issue during the Seminole Indian Wars in Florida between

358
00:27:28,521 --> 00:27:31,620
1815 and 1825.

359
00:27:31,621 --> 00:27:40,380
The musket's shotgun-like potency proved extremely effective in Florida's thick swamps.

360
00:27:40,381 --> 00:27:46,280
The shotgun's first wide use in a military role came with the American Civil War.

361
00:27:46,281 --> 00:27:50,960
Early in the war, volunteer units on both sides carried their own shotguns brought from

362
00:27:50,961 --> 00:27:52,440
home.

363
00:27:52,441 --> 00:27:58,440
Later, as ordnance improved, most troops were issued single shot and repeating rifles,

364
00:27:58,441 --> 00:28:03,440
which were much more effective against targets at distances greater than 50 yards.

365
00:28:04,440 --> 00:28:09,440
But the shotgun remained a popular weapon, especially with Confederate cavalry units

366
00:28:09,441 --> 00:28:14,440
who used double-barreled percussion shotguns with devastating effectiveness.

367
00:28:16,440 --> 00:28:20,440
The Confederate cavalry often fought in hit-and-run tactics,

368
00:28:20,441 --> 00:28:23,640
ride in, fire fast, and get out.

369
00:28:23,980 --> 00:28:27,340
And a fast-moving force like that that fought close quarters

370
00:28:27,341 --> 00:28:30,060
would find a shotgun a very valuable weapon.

371
00:28:31,320 --> 00:28:35,560
Many times a heated battle might leave a shotgun dented or bent.

372
00:28:36,200 --> 00:28:38,040
The rebels simply shortened the barrels,

373
00:28:38,380 --> 00:28:41,160
making the weapon more maneuverable during fights.

374
00:28:41,820 --> 00:28:44,100
In these cavalry confrontations,

375
00:28:44,260 --> 00:28:47,520
they just bested the Union cavalry time and time again

376
00:28:47,521 --> 00:28:50,260
because they're going to hit what they shot at,

377
00:28:50,360 --> 00:28:53,280
and when they hit something, it was going to get knocked down.

378
00:28:54,260 --> 00:28:57,160
Confederate Cavalry General Nathan Bedford Forrest

379
00:28:57,161 --> 00:29:01,140
championed the shotgun, often carrying two doubles into a fight.

380
00:29:01,960 --> 00:29:03,520
Although the South lost the war,

381
00:29:04,200 --> 00:29:06,780
Forrest never tasted defeat on the battlefield.

382
00:29:10,060 --> 00:29:13,780
After the war, the shotgun once again became a civilian weapon,

383
00:29:14,240 --> 00:29:16,860
but with the introduction of repeating shotguns,

384
00:29:16,861 --> 00:29:21,020
the US Army began using them in specialized roles.

385
00:29:23,260 --> 00:29:26,580
During an uprising in the Philippines in the early 1900s,

386
00:29:26,581 --> 00:29:29,440
Captain John Blackjack Pershing's troops

387
00:29:29,441 --> 00:29:32,460
used shotguns against the Moro nationals.

388
00:29:35,140 --> 00:29:39,980
The Moros would get themselves bound up,

389
00:29:39,981 --> 00:29:41,460
take all kinds of drugs,

390
00:29:41,461 --> 00:29:44,760
and do what they called run hurramantado,

391
00:29:44,761 --> 00:29:46,060
which, I mean, crazy,

392
00:29:46,061 --> 00:29:48,460
and they would hack, you know,

393
00:29:48,461 --> 00:29:51,200
even if they were shot with a crag rifle or something,

394
00:29:51,201 --> 00:29:52,640
they had these big knives and stuff,

395
00:29:52,641 --> 00:29:55,540
and they would hack up whoever shot them

396
00:29:55,541 --> 00:29:59,840
and before they died, and they were very difficult to stop.

397
00:29:59,841 --> 00:30:02,940
And the only thing that would stop them flat out

398
00:30:02,941 --> 00:30:04,580
was that 97 shotgun,

399
00:30:04,581 --> 00:30:07,120
so that became very popular among troops.

400
00:30:09,980 --> 00:30:12,580
When the US entered the First World War,

401
00:30:12,581 --> 00:30:15,220
Black Jack Pershing became commanding general

402
00:30:15,221 --> 00:30:17,980
of the American Expeditionary Forces.

403
00:30:17,981 --> 00:30:20,640
At his request, the Army issued a limited number

404
00:30:20,641 --> 00:30:23,760
of Bainette-equipped pump-action shotguns

405
00:30:23,761 --> 00:30:26,900
to the doughboys fighting in the trenches.

406
00:30:26,901 --> 00:30:29,320
These so-called trench guns saw action

407
00:30:29,321 --> 00:30:31,740
on night patrol duty, guard duty,

408
00:30:31,741 --> 00:30:35,160
and especially on raids into enemy territory.

409
00:30:35,161 --> 00:30:37,460
Once again, the shotgun proved itself

410
00:30:37,461 --> 00:30:39,560
a superior close-range weapon.

411
00:30:41,040 --> 00:30:43,380
They were used whenever anybody was lucky enough

412
00:30:43,381 --> 00:30:47,620
to get one issued to him as opposed to a bolt action 30.06 Springfield,

413
00:30:47,621 --> 00:30:50,580
which is a fine weapon at two or three hundred yards,

414
00:30:50,581 --> 00:30:53,620
but an almost useless weapon at ten or twenty.

415
00:30:53,621 --> 00:30:57,860
The Germans detested them, they didn't use them, they regarded them as barbaric.

416
00:30:57,861 --> 00:31:02,860
In Europe, the shotgun had remained primarily a sporting gun.

417
00:31:02,861 --> 00:31:07,340
To the uninitiated, its effect in combat was shocking.

418
00:31:07,341 --> 00:31:17,660
The German government denounced the pump action shotgun and unsuccessfully petitioned the international

419
00:31:17,661 --> 00:31:24,460
courts in an attempt to outlaw the use in warfare.

420
00:31:24,461 --> 00:31:31,920
The Germans screamed murder.

421
00:31:31,921 --> 00:31:36,480
They were using poison gas and gagging people to death

422
00:31:36,481 --> 00:31:39,200
and all forms of artillery, all this kind of thing.

423
00:31:39,201 --> 00:31:41,600
But when we came with our 97 shotguns,

424
00:31:41,601 --> 00:31:46,280
they said this is against the laws of decency.

425
00:31:46,281 --> 00:31:48,480
No country club or millionaire shooting.

426
00:31:48,481 --> 00:31:49,920
During the Second World War,

427
00:31:49,921 --> 00:31:52,760
the shotgun was again called into action.

428
00:31:52,761 --> 00:31:55,220
British and American aerial machine gunners

429
00:31:55,221 --> 00:31:57,260
trained by shooting at clay pigeons

430
00:31:57,261 --> 00:31:59,720
with shotguns from the back of trucks.

431
00:31:59,721 --> 00:32:02,840
Thus they learned how to lead a flying target

432
00:32:02,841 --> 00:32:04,420
while moving themselves.

433
00:32:09,120 --> 00:32:12,060
But the shotgun also served in combat.

434
00:32:12,061 --> 00:32:14,000
Though its use was limited in Europe,

435
00:32:14,001 --> 00:32:16,000
it proved invaluable to marines

436
00:32:16,001 --> 00:32:17,900
in the jungles of the South Pacific.

437
00:32:20,940 --> 00:32:22,380
When you're fighting in close quarters,

438
00:32:22,381 --> 00:32:24,600
you have a very short visibility.

439
00:32:24,601 --> 00:32:26,720
Generally the enemy is closer.

440
00:32:26,721 --> 00:32:34,080
Officers and men with shotguns found themselves shooting point-blank range many times at the enemy.

441
00:32:34,081 --> 00:32:40,220
And a shotgun is a perfect weapon for that because you can cycle your ammunition through there fast

442
00:32:40,221 --> 00:32:45,380
and you're getting a good dispersal of firepower in a real hurry in a short range.

443
00:32:46,740 --> 00:32:51,620
A shotgun is the most devastating short-range weapon that isn't going to kill you as well.

444
00:32:52,000 --> 00:32:54,420
It has been true since the shotgun was created.

445
00:32:54,421 --> 00:32:59,120
it's preferable to a submachine gun or anything else in terms of close-range defense.

446
00:33:00,060 --> 00:33:05,560
Since the Second World War, military shotguns have undergone a dramatic transformation.

447
00:33:06,580 --> 00:33:09,020
While the pump action is still in widespread use,

448
00:33:09,600 --> 00:33:13,040
counter-terrorist agencies and specialized military units

449
00:33:13,041 --> 00:33:17,220
now use semi-automatic and even fully automatic shotguns.

450
00:33:18,840 --> 00:33:20,860
Ammunition has changed as well.

451
00:33:20,861 --> 00:33:26,860
Lead pellets have given way to rifled slugs, gas rounds and even bean bag rounds.

452
00:33:29,560 --> 00:33:35,300
The shotgun's success in the battlefield paralleled its popularity in the private sector.

453
00:33:36,240 --> 00:33:41,420
Throughout the 20th century, the shotgun has appealed to a diverse cross-section of society,

454
00:33:42,060 --> 00:33:46,080
from Hollywood stars to bootleggers and bank robbers.

455
00:33:50,860 --> 00:34:03,200
The 1920s and 30s saw notorious American gangsters like Al Capone, John Dillinger, and Machine Gun Kelly carrying a wide range of powerful weapons, including shotguns.

456
00:34:07,900 --> 00:34:13,226
Whippet guns were small and easily concealable, similar to Sonoff's shotguns.

457
00:34:13,227 --> 00:34:16,200
The Remington Model 11 was a prime example.

458
00:34:16,201 --> 00:34:24,020
It was very popular with burglars and very popular with criminals in general.

459
00:34:24,220 --> 00:34:29,280
A sawed-off shotgun was certainly the weapon of choice, I mean even over a machine gun.

460
00:34:34,960 --> 00:34:38,220
Another weapon of choice was the automatic shotgun.

461
00:34:38,740 --> 00:34:41,560
It was fast, accurate and extremely powerful,

462
00:34:42,120 --> 00:34:45,180
capable of discharging six rounds in three seconds.

463
00:34:46,200 --> 00:34:50,640
John Browning created the first commercially successful version in 1903.

464
00:34:51,660 --> 00:34:54,440
Browning considered it one of his greatest achievements.

465
00:34:56,020 --> 00:34:59,860
Something about just the way it works, and you look at it,

466
00:34:59,920 --> 00:35:03,860
and you say, how did you ever think this up, the simple genius of it,

467
00:35:04,520 --> 00:35:08,760
that the recoil pushes the barrel back, and there's like a shock absorber,

468
00:35:08,761 --> 00:35:11,820
so it takes up the recoil itself against the shoulder.

469
00:35:12,460 --> 00:35:15,200
The barrel goes back, picks up the shell so perfectly.

470
00:35:16,200 --> 00:35:18,660
loads it, ejects the other shell.

471
00:35:21,000 --> 00:35:26,620
Socially, it was a devastating gun used by lawmen and gangsters alike.

472
00:35:27,300 --> 00:35:31,640
Bonnie Parker used one of those, and she was quite dangerous.

473
00:35:34,160 --> 00:35:38,680
To defeat the gangsters, the police had to increase their own firepower.

474
00:35:39,420 --> 00:35:42,620
Pump-action shotguns were a crucial addition to the arsenal.

475
00:35:46,200 --> 00:35:50,820
Today, the shotgun remains standard issue for police departments across America.

476
00:35:51,560 --> 00:35:57,300
It is also the weapon used for crowd control and crime fighting in densely populated areas.

477
00:35:58,580 --> 00:36:02,280
You take an automatic rifle or even an automatic pistol,

478
00:36:02,960 --> 00:36:07,480
if a round either goes through an assailant or misses entirely,

479
00:36:07,900 --> 00:36:09,820
that thing can travel on for quite a ways.

480
00:36:09,821 --> 00:36:15,820
With a limited range of a shotgun, you have more control over not only rapid firepower,

481
00:36:16,200 --> 00:36:20,820
but your deadly firepower is more controllable because it's not going to go as far,

482
00:36:21,080 --> 00:36:23,300
and you're going to keep it within the area of combat.

483
00:36:25,340 --> 00:36:31,020
This is your contemporary modern police shotgun used for controlling riots.

484
00:36:31,560 --> 00:36:35,400
The hammer is inside the weapon, so you can cock the weapon.

485
00:36:35,560 --> 00:36:38,560
It's a very smooth action. You can fire the weapon and cock it again.

486
00:36:39,060 --> 00:36:42,140
The Ithaca Model 37 is designed for law enforcement.

487
00:36:42,141 --> 00:36:45,280
and it has no disconnect, so that if you wish,

488
00:36:45,420 --> 00:36:47,760
you can simply keep your finger on the trigger

489
00:36:47,761 --> 00:36:51,720
and the gun will continue to fire each time the slide is pumped.

490
00:37:01,640 --> 00:37:05,560
But for its wide uses, the shotgun remains at its core

491
00:37:05,561 --> 00:37:08,600
what it has always been, a sporting gun.

492
00:37:09,640 --> 00:37:11,840
Skeet and track shooting have long been popular

493
00:37:11,841 --> 00:37:13,840
with people from all walks of life.

494
00:37:21,720 --> 00:37:25,520
Hollywood's golden years saw stars such as Clark Gable,

495
00:37:25,521 --> 00:37:29,020
Marlene Dietrich and Bing Crosby spending time

496
00:37:29,021 --> 00:37:30,700
